<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode>true</RestoreLockedMode>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      FS.GG.Templates#384 (root cause established by #380). `RestoreLockedMode` above
      pins each package's identity, version and CONTENT HASH — so it is only
      enforceable if exactly one archive can ever answer for a given id+version. For
      FSharp.Core that is not true by default, and this workspace resolves FSharp.Core
      implicitly through the F# SDK, so it is squarely exposed.

      The F# SDK appends its own bundled folder feed to the restore sources:

        sdk/<ver>/FSharp/Microsoft.FSharp.NetSdk.targets:69
          <PropertyGroup Condition=" '$(DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder)' != 'true' ">
            <RestoreAdditionalProjectSources>...;$(_FSharpCoreLibraryPacksFolder)</...>

      That folder ships FSharp.Core at the SDK's own version — the same 10.1.302 this
      workspace resolves — but as a DIFFERENT ARCHIVE from the one on nuget.org. Two
      byte-different packages, one id and version, both reachable in the same restore:

        nuget.org                       3066531 bytes
        sdk/10.0.302/FSharp/library-packs 3051536 bytes

      Which one gets recorded is not a property of this project: a single-project
      restore into a cold cache and a solution restore into the same cold cache can
      resolve differently. One build performs several restores, so two of them can
      disagree — and locked mode reports that disagreement as

        error NU1403: Package content hash validation failed for FSharp.Core.10.1.302.
                      The package is different than the last restore.

      which is #380 exactly, observed in the sibling fs-gg-fable-game template.
      `<clear />` in NuGet.config cannot close it: the folder arrives through
      RestoreAdditionalProjectSources (MSBuild), not through NuGet configuration, so
      nothing in NuGet.config removes it. This property is the lever that does.

      Cost of setting it: FSharp.Core is fetched from nuget.org like Expecto, instead
      of from the SDK's offline copy. This workspace already requires nuget.org for
      Expecto, so it forfeits no offline capability it otherwise had.
    -->
    <DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder>true</DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder>

    <!--
      The second half of #380, and the half that source pinning cannot reach.

      `DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder` above decides which SOURCE is consulted — but only for a
      package that must be fetched at all. NuGet's global packages folder is keyed by id+version
      alone, so once `fsharp.core/10.1.302/` is extracted there, every later restore on that machine
      uses it and validates the lock against the `contentHash` recorded in its `.nupkg.metadata`.
      Whichever build reached that folder FIRST decides which of the two archives lives there, and
      nothing a product declares can overrule an entry that already exists.

      Measured both ways. A machine whose shared folder holds nuget.org's copy restores this
      workspace green; a machine whose shared folder holds the SDK's copy fails with NU1403 — same
      product, same lock, same source pinning, opposite result. That is not a property this
      workspace can fix by naming a source, and it is why a CI runner reds while a developer
      container passes.

      So the workspace keeps its packages to itself. `RestorePackagesPath` gives it a private
      package folder, which makes the committed `contentHash` beside it enforceable on ANY machine
      regardless of what that machine's shared folder happens to hold. This is the cost of a lock
      that actually means something: packages are not shared with other checkouts, so a cold build
      downloads its own copies. A CI cache should key on `packages.lock.json` and cache
      `.nuget/packages` in the workspace.

      Removing this line does not fail loudly — it makes the workspace green on machines that agree
      with the lock and red on machines that do not, which is exactly the intermittent NU1403 #380
      spent its life chasing.
    -->
    <RestorePackagesPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory).nuget/packages</RestorePackagesPath>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
